---
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="CLI Tools and AI - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="cli-tools">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">

			<p>
				If you've used <code>gh</code> (GitHub CLI), <code>jira</code>, the Atlassian CLI, <code>gcloud</code>, <code>aws</code>, or even <code>curl</code>,
				you already know these tools are powerful but often require looking up flags and syntax.
				AI agents handle that friction naturally and can use these tools to greatly help their own workflow.
			</p>

			<h3>The AI Uses Them on Its Own</h3>

			<p>
				The real power isn't asking the AI to run a command for you. It's that the AI will use CLI tools <strong>proactively as part of its own workflow</strong> when it knows they're available.
				Tell the AI in your <a href="/task-breakdowns/agents-md">AGENTS.md</a> that <code>gh</code> is installed, and it will start reading PR descriptions, checking CI status, and pulling review comments on its own when those are relevant to the task at hand.
			</p>

			<p>
				For example, if you ask the AI to "implement the feature described in JIRA-1234," and your AGENTS.md mentions the Atlassian CLI is available, the AI will fetch the ticket, read the acceptance criteria, and use that as its spec without you copying anything. Same with GitHub: ask it to "fix the bug reported in issue #42" and it pulls the issue body, reads the stack trace, and starts working.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example AGENTS.md snippet</div>
				<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
					## Available CLI tools<br /><br />
					- `gh` is installed and authenticated. Use it to read issues, PRs, and CI status.<br />
					- Atlassian CLI (`acli`) is available. Use `acli jira workitem view TICKET-ID` to read Jira tickets.<br />
					- `gcloud` is configured for the staging project. Use it for checking service status and reading logs.
				</p>
			</div>

			<p>
				That small addition to your project config turns the AI from a code-only tool into one that can pull in context from across your entire development workflow. The AI decides when to use these tools based on what it needs, and you don't have to remember the right flags or syntax.
			</p>

			<h3>CLI Tools vs MCP Servers</h3>

			<p>
				You may have heard of <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP (Model Context Protocol)</a>{" "}
				servers as a way to connect AI to external tools. MCP provides a structured, typed interface, and services like Atlassian, Sentry, and Linear already offer MCP servers. But CLI tools have some real advantages:
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-3 my-8">
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Already installed</strong> — Most teams already have <code>gh</code>, cloud CLIs, and database tools set up.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">More capable</strong> — CLI tools typically expose the full API surface of a service. MCP servers often implement a subset of available operations.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Composable</strong> — The AI can pipe CLI output, chain commands, and combine tools in ways that MCP's structured interface doesn't support.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Well-known by the AI</strong> — Popular CLIs like <code>gh</code>, <code>gcloud</code>, and <code>aws</code> are heavily represented in training data; these tools are generally older and have more training data than MCP servers.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Lighter on context</strong> — A large MCP server loads every tool's schema into the context window before you ask anything (a full GitHub MCP server can run to tens of thousands of tokens). A CLI costs almost nothing until the AI actually runs it, which leaves more of the window free for reasoning.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				MCP still makes sense in plenty of cases: services without a good CLI, and anything where per-user OAuth, multi-tenant auth, or audit trails matter, which is why it fits enterprise, customer-facing, and regulated setups. Many teams run a hybrid, using CLI tools for day-to-day development and MCP where governance and authentication are the point. But for most everyday dev work, CLI tools are faster to set up and start using.
			</p>

			<h3>Skills for Repeated Workflows</h3>

			<p>
				When you have a CLI-based workflow the AI does regularly, or with a lesser-known CLI tool, wrap it in a{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-writing/skills">skill</a>.
				A skill packages the instructions, the right CLI commands, and your team's conventions into something reusable.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
					"Create a skill that uses the gh CLI to create a PR. It should follow our branch naming convention, write a PR description from the commit history, and add the right reviewers based on CODEOWNERS."
				</p>
			</div>

			<p>
				Many CLI tool vendors and community members have already published skills online. Before writing one from scratch, search for existing ones. And for tools with good online documentation, you can often point the AI at the docs and have it generate a working skill in a single session. Anthropic provides a{" "}
				<a href="https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/main/skills/skill-creator" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">skill creator skill</a>{" "}
				that can help you build these quickly.
			</p>

			<h3>Watch Out for Your Shell Config</h3>

			<p>
				When an agent runs a command, it usually starts a shell that sources your personal config (like <code>~/.zshrc</code>), the same one you tuned for
				interactive use. That is easy to forget, and it causes two problems. First, anything your shell prints on startup or on every <code>cd</code>{" "}
				(version-manager notices, greetings, elaborate prompts) lands in the agent's context and spends tokens on noise. A single line is nothing, but a
				session runs dozens of commands and it adds up. Second, conveniences built for humans can quietly misdirect the agent: a tool like zoxide that
				overrides <code>cd</code> to jump to your most-used directory can send the agent somewhere you did not intend, and it will then waste time
				investigating the "bug" it caused.
			</p>

			<p>
				The fix is to give agents a leaner shell. Detect whether the shell is genuinely interactive (agents run non-interactively and set environment
				variables such as <code>CLAUDECODE</code>, <code>CURSOR_AGENT</code>, or <code>CODEX_SANDBOX</code>), then gate interactive-only tools behind that
				check (custom prompts, history tricks, fuzzy finders, <code>cd</code> overrides) while leaving <code>PATH</code> and runtime setup ungated so the
				agent can still find node, rust, and the rest. This{" "}
				<a href="https://valstar.dev/blog/2026-06-13-agent-friendly-shell/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">writeup on agent-friendly shells</a>{" "}
				walks through a concrete <code>INTERACTIVE_SHELL</code> flag, and you can hand the change to your coding agent to implement.
			</p>

			<h3>Getting Started</h3>

			<p>
				The quickest way to try this: add a few lines to your <a href="/task-breakdowns/agents-md">AGENTS.md</a> listing the CLI tools available in your project, then give the AI a task that naturally requires one of them.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-teal-700 mb-1">If you use GitHub</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Add <code>gh</code> to your AGENTS.md. Then try: "Read issue #42 and implement a fix" or "Create a PR for my changes."
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-teal-700 mb-1">If you use Jira</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Set up the Atlassian CLI or MCP server (make sure to give a reference ticket number so it knows the format). Then try: "Read JIRA-1234 and implement it" or "Create a ticket for this bug we just found."
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
